(ns kotoba.compiler.accelerator-subgroup-reduce
  "The Vulkan/GLSL arm of the :subgroup-reduce kernel variant (ADR-0356,
  JUN-59; ADR-0357's ARM row reuses this same emission).

  STATE, exactly: the EMISSION is implemented here with tests and ADMITTED
  into kotoba.compiler.accelerator's plumbing (ADR-0359 decision 1):
  `lower` answers this text for the (:glsl-v1, :subgroup-reduce) pair, so
  compile-kernel seals it and verify-artifact! re-lowers it like any other
  emission. SELECTABLE since the JUN-59/JUN-60 selection rows landed in
  `kotoba.compiler.accelerator-variants` (the design home of the rows):
  `select-variant` answers :subgroup-reduce for the vendor rows under
  ADR-0356 rule 3 (this emission is the only one that admits the variant)
  and rule 6 / ADR-0357's capability precondition (a device descriptor that
  reports the fold's subgroup ops and a subgroup size dividing the
  workgroup; anything else fails closed to :baseline). The selectable rows
  are Intel (JUN-59), ARM (JUN-60), Apple (JUN-58, ADR-0360) and NVIDIA on
  the Vulkan route (JUN-57, ADR-0361, carrying the CUDA row's sm_70+ floor
  fail-closed on a reported compute capability). Nothing here is measured,
  and nothing here may be reported as optimization evidence: `basis` carries
  :measured? false and that flag flips only when an interleaved
  baseline-vs-variant comparison on an Intel device (JUN-59's measurement
  contract) replaces the basis text. On ARM the same rule holds with a Mali
  device (ADR-0357).

  What the emission is (ADR-0356's Intel row, to its letter): a reduce
  kernel whose intra-subgroup fold queries the subgroup size at RUNTIME
  (`gl_SubgroupSize`) and never bakes 32 into the fold -- Intel subgroup
  size is a device choice (8/16/32) and an emission that assumes 32 would
  be wrong on most Intel devices. The fold needs no barriers and no shared
  memory; one write barrier plus a short cross-subgroup tree over the
  gl_NumSubgroups partials replaces the baseline tree's log2(W) cascade.
  The reduction groups the same admitted elements as the :baseline tree in a
  different order -- f32 :sum agrees with the baseline to low bits on
  well-conditioned inputs, and on cancellation-sensitive inputs the
  divergence is NOT confined to the low bits and stays an UNMEASURED
  divergence regime from the baseline (ADR-0357 P1, option b); :max/:min
  are order-independent within the ordinary-value domain only (no NaN
  payloads, no negative zeros -- ADR-0356 rule 2).

  Safety condition (checked by the tests): a Vulkan subgroup size is a power
  of two, so for every KIR-admitted W (powers of two in [32, 1024]) every
  subgroup is either full or the single first partial one, and the active
  fold width is min(gl_SubgroupSize, gl_WorkGroupSize.x). The shared array
  is W floats -- the same footprint the :baseline tree uses -- and holds one
  partial per subgroup; a subgroup has at least one invocation, so
  gl_NumSubgroups <= W always.

  The ABI edge: the KIR's `n` is :runtime-u32, a uniform in the WGSL
  emission. The wire-42 (:gpu/compute) path binds storage buffers only (the
  loader-era command contract behind the removed C loader,
  tools/kexe_gpu_vulkan.c before main 36479076 removed it; the command
  survives in the sealed SPIR-V build step,
  kotoba.compiler.accelerator-spirv-compile), so this emission carries `n`
  in a read-only storage buffer at
  binding 2. Same value, same bounds semantics; the buffer-vs-uniform
  distinction is a WGSL-ism the GLSL path has no word for.

  THE WGSL ARM (`emit-wgsl`, landed 2026-10-23) is the same fold in WGSL
  spellings: `enable subgroups;` (the only enable directive -- `subgroup_id`
  and `num_subgroups` are a WGSL LANGUAGE extension, automatically available
  where the implementation supports it, and `enable subgroup_id;` is a
  shader-creation error), `subgroup_size` / `subgroup_invocation_id` /
  `subgroup_id` / `num_subgroups` builtins, `subgroupShuffleDown` for the
  fold. Spellings verified against the W3C WGSL spec and the merged gpuweb
  subgroups proposal (fetched 2026-10-23) -- vendor documentation, not a
  measurement. UNCOMPILED here: no WGSL compiler or validator exists on
  this host (the CUDA arm left this regime 2026-10-24 -- its emitted row
  compiles clean with the real nvcc on the fleet's CUDA host, the CUDA arm
  paragraph below; the WGSL arm keeps the regime), so the text contracts
  the tests pin are the whole verification, and nothing has
  executed this arm (no WebGPU execution path exists). The n of the KIR
  rides `var<uniform> n: N` at binding 2 -- the WGSL family's own choice
  (every WGSL emission binds it there); the GLSL arm's storage-buffer n is
  a wire-42 artifact with no WGSL word. P1 (option b scoping) and P2
  (subgroup IDs, never lid arithmetic) bind this arm unchanged.

  THE MSL ARM (`emit-msl`, landed 2026-10-23) is the same fold in Metal
  spellings: `[[threads_per_simdgroup]]` (Metal 2.2+; the spec's alias of
  `thread_execution_width`) is the runtime size query,
  `[[thread_index_in_simdgroup]]` / `[[simdgroup_index_in_threadgroup]]` /
  `[[simdgroups_per_threadgroup]]` the subgroup geometry,
  `simd_shuffle_down` the fold's shuffle,
  `threadgroup_barrier(mem_flags::mem_threadgroup)` the write barrier, and
  `as_type<float>` the IEEE-754 infinite neutrals. Spellings verified
  against the Apple Metal Shading Language Specification and Apple's
  Porting your Metal code to Apple silicon (fetched 2026-10-23) -- vendor
  documentation, not a measurement. One arm-level deviation FORCED by the
  language: Metal refuses a kernel named main (kernel function cannot be
  called main), so this arm's entry is the kernel's :kernel/name -- the
  ADR-0002 MSL family's own entry contract (emit-msl's) -- and the variant
  family's name-free `main` convention does not extend to Metal. That fact
  is not folklore here: the emitted source COMPILES on this host under the
  Apple Metal compiler (metal 32023.883, toolchain fact of what compiles;
  docs/evidence/gpu-subgroup-reduce-msl-20261023/), still UNMEASURED as an
  optimization claim, and no Metal execution path exists (the brokers died
  with the C loader). The KIR's n rides `constant uint& n [[buffer(2)]]`
  and the partials array is an in-kernel `threadgroup float partials[W]`
  (the WGSL arm's fixed-workgroup-array choice), not the baseline MSL
  reduce's `[[threadgroup(0)]]` pointer -- the buffer bindings (x 0, parts
  1, n 2) are the MSL family's, unchanged. P1 (option b scoping) and P2
  (subgroup IDs, never lid arithmetic) bind this arm unchanged.

  THE CUDA ARM (`emit-cuda`, landed 2026-10-23) is the same fold in CUDA
  spellings, and completes the four-emitter coverage of the fold (GLSL
  2026-10-22, WGSL and MSL 2026-10-23): the subgroup size is queried at
  RUNTIME as `warpSize` (the CUDA C++ Programming Guide's built-in device
  variable, a run-time value defined as the number of threads in a warp --
  the width is 32 on every shipping NVIDIA device and this emission names
  the spelling, not the number; the arm keeps the parametric letter of the
  others and claims none of Intel's 8/16/32 variance here),
  `threadIdx.x % sgsize` / `threadIdx.x / sgsize` are the subgroup geometry
  (CUDA has no subgroup-id builtin; the warp id IS threadIdx.x / warpSize
  -- P2's partial index, never a lid-derived fallback), the fold's shuffle
  is `__shfl_down_sync(mask, v, d, fold)` with the guide's width parameter
  carrying the runtime fold width (the guide: an out-of-range source lane
  returns the caller's own var, so the `sgid + d < fold` guard drops it
  exactly as in every arm), the participation mask is `__activemask()`
  (the runtime active-lane query -- no 32-lane constant is baked anywhere
  in the source), and the barrier is `__syncthreads()`. The guide's own
  warning on `__activemask()` (an instantaneous snapshot, not a branch
  probe) is satisfied by construction here: this kernel has no early exit
  (the padded lanes fold the identity) and every launched lane calls every
  shuffle unguarded, so the snapshot names exactly the participating
  lanes. One arm-level deviation FORCED by the family's ABI shape: the
  partials array is a fixed `__shared__ float partials[W]` (the MSL/WGSL
  arms' fixed-workgroup-array choice), not the baseline CUDA reduce's
  dynamic `extern __shared__ float s[]`. The entry point is the kernel's
  :kernel/name under the CUDA family's own extern-C kernel linkage (exactly
  what the :warp-shuffle-reduce arm emits). The KIR's n is the third kernel
  argument, the CUDA family's own ABI (x, parts, n). [COMPILED 2026-10-24,
  closing this arm's UNCOMPILED state as to one real toolchain: the whole
  admitted row of emitted sources (W in 32..1024 x :sum/:max/:min, 18
  files -- the committed corpus, re-emitted byte-identically this tick)
  compiles CLEAN with the real nvcc -- Cuda compilation tools, release
  11.4, V11.4.315 -- on the fleet's CUDA host xavier (Jetson AGX Xavier,
  L4T R35.4.1, -arch=sm_72): 18 .cubin + 18 .ptx, each PTX opening
  `.target sm_72` with the extern-C entry `reduce_{sum,max,min}`, the
  received bytes sha256-identical to the emitter's;
  docs/evidence/gpu/cuda-compile-xavier-20261024/. This host still has no
  nvcc and the WGSL arm's UNCOMPILED is untouched.] UNMEASURED UNCHANGED
  and still the operative claim: no kernel has executed this arm on any
  GPU, no timing exists, and no :measured? flag and no workgroup row moves
  -- the CUDA emitter has no execution path in this repository (wire 42
  runs Vulkan only). The text contracts the tests pin stand beside the
  compile, not replaced by it (spellings pinned to the NVIDIA CUDA C++
  Programming Guide, fetched 2026-10-23: vendor documentation, not a
  measurement). P1 (option b scoping) and P2
  (subgroup IDs, never lid arithmetic) bind this arm unchanged."
  {:kotoba/export [applicable? basis emit-cuda emit-glsl emit-wgsl emit-msl min-workgroup-size]}
  (:require [kotoba.compiler.accelerator-kir :as kir]))

(def min-workgroup-size
  "The smallest workgroup this variant admits: one full NVIDIA warp-sized
  floor shared with the :warp-shuffle-reduce row (ADR-0356's predicate). A
  smaller workgroup gets :baseline -- the fold would be over a partial
  subgroup only, where the tree is already shorter than the machinery."
  32)

(def basis
  "Why this variant exists. DESIGN ASSUMPTION, not measurement evidence.
  :measured? false is the honesty lock; it flips only when hardware
  measurement replaces :text for the row it stands on (JUN-59 on Intel,
  JUN-60 on Mali). Nothing in this repository has executed a subgroup
  reduce on either vendor's hardware."
  {:measured? false
   :text
   "Design assumption (UNMEASURED): the baseline tree reduce pays log2(W)
    barrier stages (7 at W = 128); folding within a subgroup needs no
    barriers and no shared memory, leaving one write barrier plus a
    log2(W/S)-stage cross-subgroup tree (4/3/2 stages at W = 128 for
    S = 8/16/32). Removing most of the barrier cascade is assumed to win on
    Intel hardware and on Mali (ADR-0357); no hardware has said so yet."})

(defn applicable?
  "TOTAL predicate for the row ADR-0356 specifies: a REDUCE kernel over
  :sum/:max/:min whose workgroup is a power of two in [32, 1024]. Everything
  else -- ewise, a sub-warp workgroup, a malformed or partial kernel map --
  answers false, never a throw. Selection time is where a refusal costs the
  most."
  [kir]
  (and (map? kir)
       (= :reduce (:kernel/op kir))
       (contains? #{:sum :max :min} (:kernel/operator kir))
       (let [wg (:kernel/workgroup-size kir)]
         (and (int? wg)
              (pos? wg)
              (zero? (bit-and wg (dec wg)))
              (<= wg kir/max-workgroup-size)
              (>= wg min-workgroup-size)))))

(def ^:private identity-value
  ;; The padded lanes (i >= n) fold the identity. For extrema the identity
  ;; must be the INFINITE neutral: a finite bound is corrupted by an input
  ;; that legitimately contains +Inf (:min) or -Inf (:max). GLSL has no
  ;; infinity literal; uintBitsToFloat of the IEEE-754 sign/exponent bits
  ;; is exact.
  {:sum "0.0"
   :max "uintBitsToFloat(0xFF800000u)"
   :min "uintBitsToFloat(0x7F800000u)"})

(def ^:private combine-expression
  {:sum "a+b" :max "max(a,b)" :min "min(a,b)"})

(defn emit-glsl
  "The :subgroup-reduce GLSL compute shader for one validated KIR. Total on
  the row, fail-closed off it: a kernel that `applicable?` refuses is
  refused here too (ex-info \"kernel variant not applicable\") -- silently
  answering a baseline tree would make the artifact claim a variant it does
  not carry. A malformed KIR is refused by the same validate! the ADR-0002
  emitters use. Deterministic: the same KIR lowers to the same bytes,
  forever. The entry point is `main` (the wire-42 PIPELINE op enters
  \"main\"); the kernel's :kernel/name deliberately does not appear."
  [kir]
  (kir/validate! kir)
  (when-not (applicable? kir)
    (throw (ex-info "kernel variant not applicable"
                    {:variant :subgroup-reduce
                     :op (:kernel/op kir)
                     :operator (:kernel/operator kir)
                     :workgroup-size (:kernel/workgroup-size kir)})))
  (let [wg (:kernel/workgroup-size kir)
        operator (:kernel/operator kir)
        identity (identity-value operator)
        combine (combine-expression operator)]
    (str "#version 450\n"
         ;; GLSL wants the per-feature subgroup extensions; the umbrella
         ;; GL_KHR_shader_subgroup is rejected outright by glslang
         ;; ("extension not supported"), while basic + shuffle_relative
         ;; compile -- the harness's measured-working rung patch replaces
         ;; exactly this pair (PR #1346), so emit what measures.
         "#extension GL_KHR_shader_subgroup_basic : require\n"
         "#extension GL_KHR_shader_subgroup_shuffle_relative : require\n"
         "layout(local_size_x = " wg ") in;\n"
         "layout(set = 0, binding = 0, std430) readonly buffer X { float x[]; };\n"
         "layout(set = 0, binding = 1, std430) writeonly buffer Parts { float parts[]; };\n"
         "layout(set = 0, binding = 2, std430) readonly buffer N { uint n; };\n"
         "shared float partials[" wg "];\n"
         "float combine(float a, float b) { return " combine "; }\n"
         "void main() {\n"
         "  uint i = gl_GlobalInvocationID.x;\n"
         "  float v = i < n ? x[i] : " identity ";\n"
         ;; The fold width is the ACTIVE width of this subgroup: a Vulkan
         ;; subgroup size is a power of two, so every subgroup is full or the
         ;; single first partial one, and min(S, W) is exactly the number of
         ;; active lanes. The guard drops the shuffle's undefined
         ;; out-of-range read instead of folding it in.
         "  uint fold = min(gl_SubgroupSize, gl_WorkGroupSize.x);\n"
         "  for (uint d = fold / 2u; d > 0u; d >>= 1u) {\n"
         "    float s = subgroupShuffleDown(v, d);\n"
         "    if (gl_SubgroupInvocationID + d < fold) { v = combine(v, s); }\n"
         "  }\n"
         "  if (gl_SubgroupInvocationID == 0u) { partials[gl_SubgroupID] = v; }\n"
         "  barrier();\n"
         ;; One cross-subgroup tree over the gl_NumSubgroups partials, run by
         ;; subgroup 0's lanes STRIDED by the subgroup size: the number of
         ;; pairs at stage d can exceed the subgroup width (e.g. nsub=32 with
         ;; S=8 starts at d=16), so a plain lid < d guard would leave
         ;; partials beyond the first S unfolded. nsub is 1 or a power of two
         ;; (W and S are), so the halving tree is exact. The barrier per stage
         ;; is uniform -- every invocation of the workgroup reaches it.
         "  for (uint d = gl_NumSubgroups / 2u; d > 0u; d >>= 1u) {\n"
         "    if (gl_SubgroupID == 0u) {\n"
         "      for (uint lid = gl_SubgroupInvocationID; lid < d; lid += gl_SubgroupSize) {\n"
         "        partials[lid] = combine(partials[lid], partials[lid + d]);\n"
         "      }\n"
         "    }\n"
         "    barrier();\n"
         "  }\n"
         "  if (gl_SubgroupID == 0u && gl_SubgroupInvocationID == 0u) { parts[gl_WorkGroupID.x] = partials[0]; }\n"
         "}\n")))

(def ^:private identity-value-wgsl
  ;; The same INFINITE neutrals as identity-value, in WGSL: no infinity
  ;; literal exists there either, bitcast of the IEEE-754 bits is exact --
  ;; byte-identical to accelerator.cljk's wgsl-identity (one meaning per
  ;; operator in every arm).
  {:sum "0.0"
   :max "bitcast<f32>(0xFF800000u)"
   :min "bitcast<f32>(0x7F800000u)"})

(def ^:private combine-expression-wgsl
  {:sum "a+b" :max "max(a,b)" :min "min(a,b)"})

(defn emit-wgsl
  "The :subgroup-reduce WGSL compute shader for one validated KIR. The same
  fold as emit-glsl to its letter, in WGSL spellings (the ns docstring's
  WGSL arm paragraph is the state of record). Total on the row, fail-closed
  off it -- a kernel that `applicable?` refuses is refused here too
  (ex-info \"kernel variant not applicable\"), for the same reason as
  emit-glsl. Deterministic: the same KIR lowers to the same bytes, forever.
  The entry point is `main` and :kernel/name deliberately does not appear,
  as in every other emission arm."
  [kir]
  (kir/validate! kir)
  (when-not (applicable? kir)
    (throw (ex-info "kernel variant not applicable"
                    {:variant :subgroup-reduce
                     :op (:kernel/op kir)
                     :operator (:kernel/operator kir)
                     :workgroup-size (:kernel/workgroup-size kir)})))
  (let [wg (:kernel/workgroup-size kir)
        operator (:kernel/operator kir)
        identity (identity-value-wgsl operator)
        combine (combine-expression-wgsl operator)]
    (str "enable subgroups;\n"
         "struct N { value: u32 };\n"
         "@group(0) @binding(0) var<storage,read> x: array<f32>;\n"
         "@group(0) @binding(1) var<storage,read_write> parts: array<f32>;\n"
         "@group(0) @binding(2) var<uniform> n: N;\n"
         "var<workgroup> partials: array<f32, " wg ">;\n"
         "fn combine(a:f32,b:f32)->f32{return " combine ";}\n"
         "@compute @workgroup_size(" wg ") fn main(@builtin(global_invocation_id) gid:vec3<u32>,"
         "@builtin(workgroup_id) wid:vec3<u32>,"
         "@builtin(subgroup_invocation_id) sgid:u32,"
         "@builtin(subgroup_size) sgsize:u32,"
         "@builtin(subgroup_id) sg:u32,"
         "@builtin(num_subgroups) nsg:u32){\n"
         "  let i=gid.x; var v:f32=select(" identity ",x[i],i<n.value);\n"
         ;; The fold width is the ACTIVE width of this subgroup, exactly as
         ;; in emit-glsl: a subgroup size is a power of two, so every
         ;; subgroup is full or the single first partial one, and
         ;; min(sgsize, W) is exactly the number of active lanes. The guard
         ;; drops the shuffle's indeterminate out-of-range read instead of
         ;; folding it in (WGSL: an indeterminate value is returned for an
         ;; inactive or out-of-range source invocation).
         "  let fold=min(sgsize," wg "u);\n"
         "  for(var d:u32=fold/2u;d>0u;d=d>>1u){let s=subgroupShuffleDown(v,d);if(sgid+d<fold){v=combine(v,s);}}\n"
         "  if(sgid==0u){partials[sg]=v;}\n"
         "  workgroupBarrier();\n"
         ;; The same strided cross-subgroup tree as emit-glsl (P2: the
         ;; partial index is the SUBGROUP ID, never lid arithmetic) over
         ;; nsg partials; nsg is 1 or a power of two (W and the subgroup
         ;; size are), so the halving tree is exact. The barrier per stage
         ;; is uniform -- every invocation of the workgroup reaches it.
         "  for(var e:u32=nsg/2u;e>0u;e=e>>1u){if(sg==0u){for(var lid:u32=sgid;lid<e;lid=lid+sgsize){partials[lid]=combine(partials[lid],partials[lid+e]);}}workgroupBarrier();}\n"
         "  if(sg==0u&&sgid==0u){parts[wid.x]=partials[0];}\n"
         "}\n")))

(def ^:private identity-value-msl
  ;; The same INFINITE neutrals as identity-value, in MSL: no infinity
  ;; literal exists there either, as_type of the IEEE-754 bits is exact --
  ;; byte-identical to accelerator.cljk's msl-identity (one meaning per
  ;; operator in every arm).
  {:sum "0.0f"
   :max "as_type<float>(0xFF800000u)"
   :min "as_type<float>(0x7F800000u)"})

(def ^:private combine-expression-msl
  {:sum "a+b" :max "max(a,b)" :min "min(a,b)"})

(defn emit-msl
  "The :subgroup-reduce MSL compute shader for one validated KIR. The same
  fold as emit-glsl to its letter, in Metal spellings (the ns docstring's
  MSL arm paragraph is the state of record). Total on the row, fail-closed
  off it -- a kernel that `applicable?` refuses is refused here too
  (ex-info \"kernel variant not applicable\"), for the same reason as
  emit-glsl. Deterministic: the same KIR lowers to the same bytes, forever.
  The entry point is the kernel's :kernel/name -- the ADR-0002 MSL
  family's own entry contract, because Metal REFUSES a kernel named
  `main` (compiler-verified; see the ns docstring) -- and unlike the
  GLSL/WGSL arms the name therefore appears in the source."
  [kir]
  (kir/validate! kir)
  (when-not (applicable? kir)
    (throw (ex-info "kernel variant not applicable"
                    {:variant :subgroup-reduce
                     :op (:kernel/op kir)
                     :operator (:kernel/operator kir)
                     :workgroup-size (:kernel/workgroup-size kir)})))
  (let [wg (:kernel/workgroup-size kir)
        name (:kernel/name kir)
        operator (:kernel/operator kir)
        identity (identity-value-msl operator)
        combine (combine-expression-msl operator)]
    (str "#include <metal_stdlib>\n"
         "using namespace metal;\n"
         "float combine(float a, float b) { return " combine "; }\n"
         "kernel void " name "(device const float* x [[buffer(0)]], device float* parts [[buffer(1)]], constant uint& n [[buffer(2)]], uint i [[thread_position_in_grid]], uint sgid [[thread_index_in_simdgroup]], uint sg [[simdgroup_index_in_threadgroup]], uint sgsize [[threads_per_simdgroup]], uint nsg [[simdgroups_per_threadgroup]], uint width [[threads_per_threadgroup]], uint g [[threadgroup_position_in_grid]]) {\n"
         "  threadgroup float partials[" wg "];\n"
         "  float v = i < n ? x[i] : " identity ";\n"
         ;; The fold width is the ACTIVE width of this SIMD-group, exactly
         ;; as in emit-glsl: a Metal SIMD-group size is a power of two, so
         ;; every SIMD-group is full or the single first partial one, and
         ;; min(sgsize, width) is exactly the number of active lanes. The
         ;; guard drops the shuffle's indeterminate out-of-range read
         ;; instead of folding it in (simd_shuffle_down does not wrap: the
         ;; lower delta lanes keep their data and upper-lane reads are
         ;; indeterminate).
         "  uint fold = min(sgsize, width);\n"
         "  for (uint d = fold / 2u; d > 0u; d >>= 1u) {\n"
         "    float s = simd_shuffle_down(v, d);\n"
         "    if (sgid + d < fold) { v = combine(v, s); }\n"
         "  }\n"
         "  if (sgid == 0u) { partials[sg] = v; }\n"
         "  threadgroup_barrier(mem_flags::mem_threadgroup);\n"
         ;; The same strided cross-subgroup tree as emit-glsl (P2: the
         ;; partial index is the SUBGROUP index, never lid arithmetic) over
         ;; nsg partials; nsg is 1 or a power of two (width and the
         ;; SIMD-group size are), so the halving tree is exact. The barrier
         ;; per stage is uniform -- every thread of the threadgroup reaches
         ;; it.
         "  for (uint d = nsg / 2u; d > 0u; d >>= 1u) {\n"
         "    if (sg == 0u) {\n"
         "      for (uint lid = sgid; lid < d; lid += sgsize) {\n"
         "        partials[lid] = combine(partials[lid], partials[lid + d]);\n"
         "      }\n"
         "    }\n"
         "    threadgroup_barrier(mem_flags::mem_threadgroup);\n"
         "  }\n"
         "  if (sg == 0u && sgid == 0u) { parts[g] = partials[0]; }\n"
         "}\n")))

(def ^:private identity-value-cuda
  ;; The same INFINITE neutrals as identity-value, in CUDA C: __uint_as_float
  ;; is exact and header-free -- byte-identical to accelerator.cljk's
  ;; cuda-identity (one meaning per operator in every arm).
  {:sum "0.0f"
   :max "__uint_as_float(0xFF800000u)"
   :min "__uint_as_float(0x7F800000u)"})

(def ^:private combine-expression-cuda
  {:sum "a+b" :max "fmaxf(a,b)" :min "fminf(a,b)"})

(defn emit-cuda
  "The :subgroup-reduce CUDA C++ compute kernel for one validated KIR. The
  same fold as emit-glsl to its letter, in CUDA spellings (the ns
  docstring's CUDA arm paragraph is the state of record). Total on the row,
  fail-closed off it -- a kernel that `applicable?` refuses is refused here
  too (ex-info \"kernel variant not applicable\"), for the same reason as
  emit-glsl. Deterministic: the same KIR lowers to the same bytes, forever.
  The entry point is the kernel's :kernel/name under the CUDA family's own
  extern-C kernel linkage (exactly what emit-cuda-shuffle-reduce emits); the
  min(S, W) fold width is spelled as a ternary -- the CUDA family emits no
  math-header names for integer min, and the shuffle arm avoids min the same
  way."
  [kir]
  (kir/validate! kir)
  (when-not (applicable? kir)
    (throw (ex-info "kernel variant not applicable"
                    {:variant :subgroup-reduce
                     :op (:kernel/op kir)
                     :operator (:kernel/operator kir)
                     :workgroup-size (:kernel/workgroup-size kir)})))
  (let [wg (:kernel/workgroup-size kir)
        name (:kernel/name kir)
        operator (:kernel/operator kir)
        identity (identity-value-cuda operator)
        combine (combine-expression-cuda operator)]
    (str "__device__ float combine(float a, float b) { return " combine "; }\n"
         "extern \"C\" __global__ void " name "(const float* x, float* parts, unsigned int n){\n"
         "  __shared__ float partials[" wg "];\n"
         "  unsigned int i = blockIdx.x*blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;\n"
         "  unsigned int sgsize = warpSize;\n"
         "  unsigned int sgid = threadIdx.x % sgsize;\n"
         "  unsigned int sg = threadIdx.x / sgsize;\n"
         "  float v = i < n ? x[i] : " identity ";\n"
         ;; The fold width is the ACTIVE width of this subgroup, exactly as
         ;; in emit-glsl: min(S, W), both powers of two in the admitted row
         ;; (and the shuffle's width parameter carries it -- the guide's
         ;; width partitions the warp into fold-sized subsections with
         ;; logical lane IDs from 0, so a partial first subgroup folds
         ;; among its own active lanes). The guard drops the shuffle's
         ;; out-of-range read (the guide: the caller's own var is returned)
         ;; instead of folding it in.
         "  unsigned int fold = sgsize < blockDim.x ? sgsize : blockDim.x;\n"
         "  unsigned int mask = __activemask();\n"
         "  for (unsigned int d = fold / 2u; d > 0u; d >>= 1u) {\n"
         "    float s = __shfl_down_sync(mask, v, d, fold);\n"
         "    if (sgid + d < fold) { v = combine(v, s); }\n"
         "  }\n"
         "  if (sgid == 0u) { partials[sg] = v; }\n"
         "  __syncthreads();\n"
         ;; The same strided cross-subgroup tree as emit-glsl (P2: the
         ;; partial index is the SUBGROUP index -- threadIdx.x / warpSize,
         ;; CUDA's own spelling of the warp id -- never lid arithmetic)
         ;; over nsg partials; nsg is 1 or a power of two (W and the
         ;; subgroup size are), so the halving tree is exact. The barrier
         ;; per stage is uniform -- every thread of the block reaches it.
         "  unsigned int nsg = (blockDim.x + sgsize - 1) / sgsize;\n"
         "  for (unsigned int d = nsg / 2u; d > 0u; d >>= 1u) {\n"
         "    if (sg == 0u) {\n"
         "      for (unsigned int lid = sgid; lid < d; lid += sgsize) {\n"
         "        partials[lid] = combine(partials[lid], partials[lid + d]);\n"
         "      }\n"
         "    }\n"
         "    __syncthreads();\n"
         "  }\n"
         "  if (sg == 0u && sgid == 0u) { parts[blockIdx.x] = partials[0]; }\n"
         "}\n")))
